menu "PID Controller Configuration"

    # ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    # All three gains (Kp, Ki, Kd) are stored as integer thousandths so
    # menuconfig only deals with whole numbers. The PID component divides
    # by 1000 at startup. Examples:
    #     value 300 -> coefficient 0.300
    #     value  20 -> coefficient 0.020
    #     value   5 -> coefficient 0.005
    #
    # Output limits stay as plain percent (value 0..100 = 0..100 % duty).
    # ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    config PID_KP
        int "Kp - Proportional gain (thousandths; 300 = 0.300)"
        default 115
        range 0 1000
        help
            Proportional gain, stored as Kp x 1000. Example: 300 = Kp 0.300.

            FORMULA
              P_term = Kp * (setpoint - measured)

            INTUITION
              "How hard the controller pushes the heater RIGHT NOW based on
              how far off we are." It's a spring: the further you stretch
              it (= bigger error), the harder it pulls back.

            CONCRETE EXAMPLE (Kp = 0.300, output_max = 1.00)
              error =  1 C  -> P_term = 0.30  -> 30 % power
              error =  3 C  -> P_term = 0.90  -> 90 % power
              error =  5 C  -> P_term = 1.50  -> clamped to 100 % power
              error = 50 C  -> P_term = 15.0  -> clamped to 100 % power
              => Anything past ~3 C of lag saturates the heater. That is
                 EXPECTED on a cold-start; the saturation only matters once
                 you're near setpoint.

            IF YOU INCREASE Kp
              + Faster reaction to disturbances (door opens, load added).
              + Smaller steady-state offset while holding.
              - More tendency to overshoot the setpoint.
              - Eventually starts ringing/oscillating around setpoint.

            IF YOU DECREASE Kp
              + Smoother, calmer output.
              - Slower ramp tracking, the chamber lags the setpoint more.
              - Holds further below setpoint (needs higher Ki to compensate).

            CLASSIC SYMPTOMS
              Too low : "It just won't reach setpoint — sits 5 C low forever."
              Too high: "It overshoots by 20 C then keeps bouncing."

            TUNING TIP
              Standard recipe: with Ki = 0 and Kd = 0, raise Kp until the
              chamber oscillates around setpoint. Cut Kp by ~30 %. Now add
              Kd to brake overshoot, then small Ki to close the residual gap.
              Typical furnace range: 100 .. 500 (i.e. Kp 0.10 .. 0.50).

    config PID_KI
        int "Ki - Integral gain (thousandths; 20 = 0.020)"
        default 1
        range 0 1000
        help
            Integral gain, stored as Ki x 1000. Example: 20 = Ki 0.020.

            FORMULA
              I_term = Ki * sum_over_time( error * dt )

            INTUITION
              "Keep score of how long we've been off-target, and slowly
              add output to close any remaining gap." It's the only term
              that can drive STEADY-STATE error to zero.

            CONCRETE EXAMPLE (Ki = 0.020, dt = 1 s)
              error  1 C held for 60 s  -> integral = 60
                                        -> I_term = 0.020 * 60 = 1.20
                                        -> would saturate output 100 %
              error  0.5 C held for 60 s -> I_term = 0.60 -> 60 % power
              => Even a "small" Ki winds up substantial output over a minute.
                 Start small.

            IF YOU INCREASE Ki
              + Closes the residual offset (chamber holds AT setpoint, not
                "near it") much faster.
              - Tends to OVERSHOOT after a setpoint change because the
                integrator keeps winding past the goal.
              - Can cause slow oscillation (much slower than Kp oscillation).

            IF YOU DECREASE Ki
              + Cleaner step response, less overshoot.
              - Steady-state offset persists. You'll sit at e.g. 197 C
                instead of 200 C indefinitely.

            CLASSIC SYMPTOMS
              Too low : "Holds steady but 3 C below setpoint forever."
              Too high: "Reaches setpoint then floats up to setpoint+5 C
                         over the next 2 minutes before coming back."

            ANTI-WINDUP
              The code uses conditional integration: while the output is
              saturated AND the error would push it further into saturation,
              the integral is NOT advanced. So a long cold-start cannot make
              the integrator explode. You can run with non-zero Ki safely.

            TUNING TIP
              Add LAST. Get Kp and Kd doing useful work first, then add
              just enough Ki to eliminate the residual hold offset. If you
              see oscillation appear when you raise Ki, you're too high.
              Typical furnace range: 5 .. 30 (i.e. Ki 0.005 .. 0.030).

    config PID_KD
        int "Kd - Derivative gain (thousandths; 50 = 0.050)"
        default 30
        range 0 1000
        help
            Derivative gain, stored as Kd x 1000. Example: 50 = Kd 0.050.

            FORMULA  (derivative-on-measurement, not on error)
              D_term = -Kd * d(measured) / dt

            INTUITION
              "Look at how fast the chamber temperature is RISING right now.
              The faster it's rising, the harder we should brake — before
              the rise carries us past the setpoint." This is exactly the
              mechanism that catches the stored heat in glowing elements.

            WHY derivative-on-MEASUREMENT (not on error)
              If we did d(error)/dt, a sudden setpoint jump would generate
              a huge negative spike (error step = giant derivative). On
              measurement, we ignore setpoint jumps and only react to
              actual temperature movement. Much smoother.

            CONCRETE EXAMPLE (Kd = 0.050)
              Climbing 0.5 C/s  -> D_term = -0.025 -> subtracts 2.5 % power
              Climbing 1.0 C/s  -> D_term = -0.050 -> subtracts 5 % power
              Climbing 3.0 C/s  -> D_term = -0.150 -> subtracts 15 % power
              => The faster the chamber heats, the more aggressively the
                 PID pulls back. This activates BEFORE error reaches zero.

            IF YOU INCREASE Kd
              + Less overshoot at the top of fast ramps.
              + Earlier braking against thermal inertia.
              - Sensor noise (thermocouple jitter) gets amplified into
                output noise. Watch for the SSR clicking rapidly.

            IF YOU DECREASE Kd
              + Smoother output, less affected by sensor noise.
              - More overshoot when the chamber is rising fast and
                arriving at setpoint.

            CLASSIC SYMPTOMS
              Too low : "We always overshoot the top of a ramp by 10 C."
              Too high: "Output is jittery — power flickers between 40 %
                         and 60 % every second."

            TUNING TIP
              Useful range for a thermal-mass furnace: 20 .. 150
              (i.e. Kd 0.020 .. 0.150). If output gets jittery, halve it.
              If overshoot persists, double it. Goes well with a clean
              filtered thermocouple reading; if your sensor is noisy,
              keep Kd modest.

    config PID_OUTPUT_MIN
        int "Output minimum (% duty)"
        default 0
        range 0 100
        help
            Lower clamp on PID output, in PERCENT of full SSR duty.

            Almost always 0. Raise this only if you have a specific reason
            for the heater to remain partly on at all times (e.g. preventing
            element thermal cycling), which is unusual for a furnace.

    config PID_OUTPUT_MAX
        int "Output maximum (% duty)"
        default 100
        range 0 100
        help
            Upper clamp on PID output, in PERCENT of full SSR duty.

            100 % is the right default for healthy hardware: let the PID
            command everything available when it needs to. Cap below 100 %
            only for a deliberate physical reason (e.g. a damaged SSR or
            element you don't trust at full duty). Capping below what the
            PID actually wants will make the controller fall behind at
            high temperatures, because radiative loss grows fast with
            absolute temperature — at 400 C the chamber loses heat much
            faster than at 100 C, so the steady-state power needed to hold
            is much higher.

    config PID_ADAPTIVE_OUTPUT_LIMIT_ENABLED
        bool "Enable adaptive output limit near setpoint"
        default n
        help
            EXPERIMENTAL / OFF BY DEFAULT.

            WHAT IT DOES
              Shrinks the UPPER output clamp as |error| shrinks (gain-scheduled
              by how far the measured temp is from the setpoint). Far from
              setpoint the full PID_OUTPUT_MAX is allowed; near setpoint output
              is capped at a small fraction (the bands/factors below). The aim
              is to stop the elements being driven hard right before arrival, so
              their stored heat cannot coast the chamber past setpoint.

            WHICH SETPOINT DOES IT MEASURE AGAINST? (important)
              The CURRENT, MOVING ramp setpoint — NOT the stage's end target.
              During a HEATING stage the profile feeds the PID a setpoint that
              ramps linearly from the stage start temp up to the target, and
              this clamp keys off |moving_setpoint − measured|. So if a stage's
              end target is 100 C but the ramp is currently at 70.4 C, the band
              uses 70.4, not 100.

            DOES THIS FIX STAGE-CHANGE / TOP-OF-RAMP OVERSHOOT?
              Partly, but bluntly — and with a side effect you must understand.
              Because it keys off the MOVING setpoint, a well-tracked ramp keeps
              |error| small (the chamber follows the ramp within a degree or
              two) for the WHOLE climb. So this clamp throttles output across
              the entire ramp, not just at the final approach — the ramp then
              lags or stair-steps because the heater is pinned to the "hold"
              fraction while it is still meant to be climbing. It can cut
              overshoot, but it tends to strangle ramp tracking at the same
              time. It is a hard CLAMP, not a smooth brake.

            PREFER Kd FOR OVERSHOOT
              The clean fix for thermal-inertia overshoot is derivative gain
              (PID_KD). Being derivative-on-measurement, it subtracts power in
              proportion to how fast the chamber is RISING, easing off the
              elements BEFORE error reaches zero. Unlike this clamp, a steady
              ramp produces a steady, modest D offset (constant rise rate)
              rather than a throttled ceiling — so Kd brakes the arrival without
              choking the climb. Reach for Kd first (your field notes already
              trend that way: Kd=5 on the 25 kW unit).

            WHEN TO USE THIS INSTEAD
              Only if a single Kd genuinely cannot tame overshoot across the
              whole range AND raising Kd makes the SSR chatter. In a narrow band
              like this furnace (<=200 C) one Kd value should cover it, so you
              will likely never need this. If you do enable it, gate it to
              HOLDING only (see the commented pid_controller_set_adaptive_enabled
              call in the coordinator) so it never throttles a ramp.

            DO NOT combine with feedforward: near setpoint this clamp can cut
            the feedforward base power itself and reintroduce droop.

            When OFF (default), the static PID_OUTPUT_MAX is used at all
            times and the bands/factors below are ignored.

    config PID_ADAPTIVE_BAND_FAR_C
        int "Adaptive: full-power band threshold (deg C)"
        default 20
        range 1 500
        depends on PID_ADAPTIVE_OUTPUT_LIMIT_ENABLED
        help
            When |error| >= this many degrees C, the full configured
            PID_OUTPUT_MAX is allowed.

    config PID_ADAPTIVE_BAND_MID_C
        int "Adaptive: mid-band threshold (deg C)"
        default 10
        range 1 500
        depends on PID_ADAPTIVE_OUTPUT_LIMIT_ENABLED

    config PID_ADAPTIVE_BAND_NEAR_C
        int "Adaptive: near-band threshold (deg C)"
        default 5
        range 1 500
        depends on PID_ADAPTIVE_OUTPUT_LIMIT_ENABLED

    config PID_ADAPTIVE_BAND_CLOSE_C
        int "Adaptive: close-band threshold (deg C)"
        default 2
        range 1 500
        depends on PID_ADAPTIVE_OUTPUT_LIMIT_ENABLED

    config PID_ADAPTIVE_FACTOR_MID_PCT
        int "Adaptive: mid-band output factor (% of OUTPUT_MAX)"
        default 70
        range 0 100
        depends on PID_ADAPTIVE_OUTPUT_LIMIT_ENABLED

    config PID_ADAPTIVE_FACTOR_NEAR_PCT
        int "Adaptive: near-band output factor (% of OUTPUT_MAX)"
        default 40
        range 0 100
        depends on PID_ADAPTIVE_OUTPUT_LIMIT_ENABLED

    config PID_ADAPTIVE_FACTOR_CLOSE_PCT
        int "Adaptive: close-band output factor (% of OUTPUT_MAX)"
        default 25
        range 0 100
        depends on PID_ADAPTIVE_OUTPUT_LIMIT_ENABLED

    config PID_ADAPTIVE_FACTOR_HOLD_PCT
        int "Adaptive: hold-band output factor (% of OUTPUT_MAX)"
        default 15
        range 0 100
        depends on PID_ADAPTIVE_OUTPUT_LIMIT_ENABLED
        help
            When |error| is below all band thresholds (we're "on setpoint"),
            output is capped at this fraction of OUTPUT_MAX. Keep this
            non-zero so the controller can still supply enough heat to
            offset steady-state losses.

    # ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    # Feedforward (steady-state power model)
    #
    # The calibration points below are intentionally NOT "depends on" the
    # enable switch: the same model is also used to seed the integrator on a
    # bumpless restart (pid_controller_reset_for_setpoint), even when the
    # additive feedforward term itself is left off. With the inert defaults
    # (both percentages 0) the model returns 0, so behaviour is identical to a
    # plain PID until you calibrate it.
    # ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    config PID_FEEDFORWARD_ENABLED
        bool "Enable feedforward (add steady-state power model to output)"
        default n
        help
            OFF BY DEFAULT. Even when ON, behaviour is unchanged until the two
            calibration points below are set to non-zero percentages.

            FORMULA
              FF = interp(setpoint; (T_LOW -> PCT_LOW), (T_HIGH -> PCT_HIGH))
              Out = P_term + I_term + D_term + FF        (then clamped)
              (flat-clamped to PCT_LOW below T_LOW and PCT_HIGH above T_HIGH.)

            INTUITION
              "Before the feedback loop does anything, hand the heater the duty
              we ALREADY KNOW it takes to sit at this temperature." The PID then
              only has to trim the small leftover error instead of discovering
              the entire hold power from scratch with the integrator.

            WHY IT HELPS
              Furnace heat loss rises with temperature, so the duty to HOLD
              180 C is higher than to hold 100 C. Without feedforward only the
              integral can supply that base power, and it must wind up from zero
              every time -> the chamber sits below setpoint ("droop") and lags
              on ramps while the integral catches up. Feedforward supplies the
              base power instantly, so the integral hovers near zero and
              droop/lag shrink markedly.

            HOW TO CALIBRATE  (do this once per furnace, like the Kp/Ki/Kd recipe)
              You are simply measuring "what steady duty holds each temperature."
              1. Leave both percentages at 0 (model inert) and run a normal HOLD
                 at a LOW temperature in your range, e.g. 100 C. Let it fully
                 settle (no more drift).
              2. Read the steady "Out:" value from the PID log line. Out is a
                 fraction 0..1, so "Out: 0.12" means 12 %. Watch it for a minute
                 and take the value it hovers around. That is PCT_LOW at
                 PID_FF_T_LOW_C (e.g. T_LOW=100, PCT_LOW=12).
              3. Repeat at a HIGH temperature in your range, e.g. 180 C, and
                 read the steady Out (e.g. 0.28 -> PCT_HIGH=28 at T_HIGH=180).
              4. Enter the two points, set PID_FEEDFORWARD_ENABLED=y, rebuild.
              5. Re-run a hold. In the log the "I:" term should now sit near 0
                 and the chamber should reach setpoint faster with less droop.
                 If it now floats slightly ABOVE setpoint while holding, your
                 percentages are a touch high — trim them by 1-2 points.

            EXAMPLE FOR THIS FURNACE  (operates <=200 C, usually <=150 C)
              Pick the two points to STRADDLE where you actually run so the
              interpolation is accurate there. Good bracket: T_LOW=100,
              T_HIGH=200, with PCT_LOW/PCT_HIGH measured at those temps. At
              150 C the model then interpolates halfway between the two duties.
              Below 100 C it clamps flat to PCT_LOW, above 200 C to PCT_HIGH
              (you never go there). Because your span is narrow and low, the
              two duties will be fairly close and even a rough calibration helps.

            NOTES
              - Inert defaults (PCT 0/0) == identical to a plain PID, so it is
                safe to leave enabled-but-uncalibrated.
              - DO NOT combine with the adaptive output ceiling: near setpoint
                the adaptive clamp can throttle the feedforward base power itself
                and reintroduce the droop feedforward removes.
              - Feedforward and Kd are COMPLEMENTARY: feedforward sets the steady
                base power, Kd brakes the approach to limit overshoot.

    config PID_FF_T_LOW_C
        int "Feedforward: low calibration temperature (deg C)"
        default 100
        range 0 2000
        help
            Temperature of the low calibration point. PID_FF_PCT_LOW is the
            steady hold duty observed at this temperature. Pick a value near
            the LOW end of where you actually run (this furnace: ~100 C).

    config PID_FF_PCT_LOW
        int "Feedforward: hold duty at low temp (% of full SSR duty)"
        default 0
        range 0 100
        help
            Steady-state % duty needed to HOLD PID_FF_T_LOW_C, read from the
            PID log "Out:" value once settled (Out 0.12 -> enter 12). Default 0
            makes the model inert; set it from a field measurement to enable.

    config PID_FF_T_HIGH_C
        int "Feedforward: high calibration temperature (deg C)"
        default 200
        range 0 2000
        help
            Temperature of the high calibration point. PID_FF_PCT_HIGH is the
            steady hold duty observed at this temperature. Pick a value near
            the HIGH end of where you actually run (this furnace: ~200 C).

    config PID_FF_PCT_HIGH
        int "Feedforward: hold duty at high temp (% of full SSR duty)"
        default 0
        range 0 100
        help
            Steady-state % duty needed to HOLD PID_FF_T_HIGH_C. Default 0 makes
            the model inert; set it from a field measurement to enable.
endmenu
